  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/2/2f/X-Men_Vol_1_1.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Professor X summons his X-Men (who are revealed to be \"students\") and proceeds to put them through some basic training exercises, giving us the chance to see what they can do.&nbsp; Angel says \"Mistakes are for homo-sapiens,\" clueing us in that there's something different about them that we've not been told yet.&nbsp; Iceman is revealed to be 16, a \"couple of years younger than the others\" (and is indeed referred to as \"Junior\" by Cyclops).&nbsp; Beast is revealed to be the oldest.&nbsp; When Cyclops attacks Iceman as part of the training, Iceman cries \"You're openin' that cotton-pickin' visor of yours wider!\"\n\nProfessor X then calls for them to cease, for at that moment, a new student is arriving, \"a most attractive young lady!\"&nbsp; Heh, the top of page 8 has three of them admiring the \"redhead\" and \"living doll\" from the window.&nbsp; When Jean Grey comes in to ask the professor why she was invited to join the school, we learn about \"mutants,\" who possess \"ex-tra power.\"&nbsp; The 5 students are then finally introduced and given actual names, except that Scott is referred to as \"Slim Summers.\"&nbsp; Jean reveals her powers while the Professor gives his own background, speculating that he is perhaps the first mutant (although note that this very same speculation was also said of the Sub-Mariner in the Fantastic Four annual, published on the <i>same day</i> - see above).&nbsp; He also reveals that he's in the chair due to \"a childhood accident.\"\n\nIt's then that the professor also reveals that their mission is to protect the Earth from the \"evil mutants.\"&nbsp; And on cue, the scene shifts to Magneto, who is the first to refer to \"Homo Superior\" and clue us in that he doesn't think the human race deserves to have dominion over the planet any longer - \"the day of the <i>mutants</i> is upon us!\"\n\nAnd soon, at the missile launching site called \"Cape Citadel,\" a missile veers off target, and guns and tanks begin moving by themselves, all controlled by Magneto, and he leaves a note in the sky, taking credit for the mischief.&nbsp; Then he shows up at the base personally to claim it \"in the name of Homo Superior!\"&nbsp; (Kirby gives us a nice close-up of his eyes through his helmet at the bottom of page 15 - very menacing!)\n\nProfessor X summons the X-Men, telling them \"this will be your baptism by fire\" and to go there and defeat \"the first of the evil mutants!\"&nbsp; They are driven to the airport in the professor's Rolls Royce and flown to the Cape in his private jet.&nbsp; (The team is referred to as \"the X-Men and Marvel Girl\" in the text.)\n\nCyclops introduces himself to the commander and orders the X-Men to attack - clearly he's been given the lead here.&nbsp; They penetrate Magneto's shield (with <i>another</i> fantastic panel by Kirby to open up page 19).&nbsp; But Magneto strikes back immediately (not yet realizing that the super-powered attack is also mutant-based) - he fires missiles at them.&nbsp;  However, by page 22, when he personally goes to attack them, he has figured out that they too are mutants and yet declares \"Not all those with mutant powers are fit to rule the Earth.&nbsp; <i>You</i> must be destroyed!\"\n\nBut his attempt to kill the youths fails, and he heads off to fight another day.&nbsp; The cape commander thanks the X-Men, and the professor rejoices that the hours of training were worth it as they fly back to re-join him.",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "The concept of \"mutants\" was hinted at in this same month's issues, but we're given the full introduction here.&nbsp; It's clear this book will be set apart from the others, as the X-Men have a very unique purpose, which is that of protecting mutants from humans while at the same time protecting humans from mutants of the more evil variety.&nbsp; This, and the concept of the school for the training of young mutants is what manages to elevate the rating on this issue to upper-mid-grade.&nbsp; It's an interesting backdrop and worthy of a solid grade.&nbsp; It leaves us with the sense that we are indeed going to be getting an all-different point-of-view here, which could be very intriguing.\n\nThe issue's obvious flaw was how quickly Magneto turned against his own kind - the \"homo superior\" he was at first claiming that it was time for.&nbsp; However, later on, he seemed more annoyed that any of them could possibly disagree with him wanting to take over...and then surprisingly decided to kill them on the spot!&nbsp; How <i>dare </i>they spoil his plans of world conquest - the <i>brats</i>!&nbsp; For a character that is otherwise unique, this decision on his part seemed to come right out of the \"super villain playbook\" which I was disappointed to see.&nbsp; As a result, the book gets solid, but not outstanding, marks.\n\nIn the interest of full disclosure, I must point out that I have never been a big X-Men fan.&nbsp; I began reading comics when Claremont and Byrne were in the midst of their historic run on the title in the late 1970s and did indeed enjoy those issues, but when that ended, the book's direction gradually went south, and I ultimately tired of it (and it was in fact one of the first titles I <i>stopped</i> reading).&nbsp; However, as a comics collector at the time, you had no choice but to keep buying it because <i>that's just what you had to do</i>, whether you liked the title or <i>not</i>.&nbsp; It is my opinion that this fear on the part of collectors was ultimately more responsible for why it became a #1 seller than the content, but that's another story).&nbsp; I confess I have not read many of the group's early adventures, so I look forward to that and will try to keep an open mind, as my mission here is to experience these issues in the order they were released to the public.\n\nFor the record (in case anyone cares), I have always pronounced Magneto's name with the short E sound, because that's how the word \"magnet\" is pronounced.&nbsp; He is like a human \"magnet\" and there is nothing \"neat-o\" about him.&nbsp; I understand that various TV shows and/or movies have the character's name pronounced differently, but I can hardly be blamed for their error, so you'll only hear the <i>correct</i> pronunciation here, <i>heh</i>!&nbsp; (I might also point out that in response to Ronald Ford's letter published in [LINK:1966:XMEN_29|XMEN #29] in 1966, Stan writes \"<i>Personally, we really dig Magnetty...</i>\"&nbsp; Clearly this demonstrates that Stan also pronounced the name with a short E - let's not pretend otherwise, or he would have written \"<i>Magneety</i><i>.</i>\")\n\n\nAnd for <i>really</i> old-timers out there, I got a kick out of Michael Havery's letter published in TOS #62 where he points out that a character named \"Magneto\" appeared in <i>Strange Tales</i> #84, with a cover date of May, 1961, and wouldn't you know it, he's <i>right!</i>  I pulled up that old issue, and right there on the cover, in big bold letters reads:&nbsp; <i>This is...MAGNETO!&nbsp; The strangest menace the world has ever known!!!</i>  Wow, that is funny!&nbsp; And the editors admit he has them \"dead to rights,\" further pointing out that <i>Boy, you must have been reading our mags for centuries</i>!\n\nNote that this wouldn't be the last time the ST #84 issue was mentioned.&nbsp; As late as 1967, readers were still pointing this out.&nbsp; See Stuart Henderson's letter published in [LINK:1967:XMEN_36|XMEN #36], for example.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nFIXME - write something",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}